this is a production of Cornell  University  engaged learning is a key component of  our mission at Cornell University as we  prepare students to be creative  collaborative and critically reflective  leaders we take that really seriously in  horticulture where we engage students in  three important ways we provide students  with direct hands-on experiences we also  give students chances to apply what  they've learned in communities from the  community of the classroom to local and  global settings and we provide students  with opportunities to reflect on their  learning to consider and articulate how  they will use what they've learned to  make the world a better place as they  grow to become leaders in horticulture  so what does engaged learning look like  in horticulture let's look at some  examples students in creating the urban  Eden serve the campus community while  they learn they design and install  landscape plantings as part of their  intensive study of more than 300 species  of trees shrubs and vines including this  runoff filtering bioswale along Tower  Road we all enjoy the visual impact of  their work walking across this beautiful  campus in horticultural science and  systems students delve into many  hands-on activities from planting trees  to harvesting peppers to donating to a  local food bank they sample food remove  invasive plants from natural areas and  create textile art with plant-based dies  every fall students in restoration  ecology take on a real-world project in  the local community working together to  gather data analyze the issues and  report their findings  one year they investigated  cost-effective options for using  sediment from future dredging in the  cave the lake inlet to promote ecosystem  services the experiential learning  students gained in the field is not  separate from their classroom experience  rather it builds on it agricultural  science majors and many plant science  majors complete a summer internship to  explore career interests they apply  coursework to real-world problems and  network with growers and agricultural  professionals as well as K through 12  audiences Master Gardener volunteers and  others some create blogs to reflect on  their experiences both what they learned  and then how it changed them viticulture  and enology majors are also engaged in  all phases of growing grapes and making  wine from planting pruning and managing  a 500 vine organic vineyard near campus  to harvesting the grapes and bottling of  fruits of their labor in my course the  art of horticulture students get  first-hand experience with a range of  plant-based materials and media choosing  one for an intensive creative project in  class experiences strengthen the link to  learning and exercises and writing  encourages students to reflect on the  impact of nature and gardens on their  well-being we create public art which  may range from Assad sofa on campus to  special installations such as the  large-scale stick work willow sculpture  at the Ithaca Children's Garden budding  student researchers frequently are  guided in individual studies of their  own design by faculty mentors students  have worked with one professor to grow  vegetable transplants for distribution  to low-income family  to encourage home gardening and healthy  diets others have worked in communities  through cooperative extension programs  ranging from school garden partnerships  with elementary schools to working with  farmers and commercial growers hands on  horticulture instills non-majors with a  lifelong appreciation for plants and  teaches them basic horticultural skills  they apply techniques such as pruning to  campus landscapes and fruit trees at the  cornell orchards and students in a  Freshman Seminar have gotten their hands  dirty partnering with Habitat for  Humanity to see lawns for low-income  housing but Cornell is a global  university and the communities in which  our students work extend far beyond  Ithaca in my course experiential garden  based learning and Belize the students  spend their entire Spring Break creating  school gardens and teaching lessons that  tie the garden to the curriculum and  southern Belize so by focusing on those  three things direct experience  reflection and community engagement  we're preparing our students to become  leaders in their field in their  communities and all around the world  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu
 

